WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.700
The minivan was designed with families in
mind, but it also revolutionized travel for

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.170
people in wheelchairs.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.780
Retrofitted for wheelchair accessibility.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.930
The minivan offers unprecedented mobility.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.340
No need to wait for special wheelchair
transportation when there's a minivan in the

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:25.030
driveway. Convert a minivan and change a
life.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.830
A wheelchair accessibility conversion offers
the convenience of a ride.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:37.000
Any time from the outside, the van will look
no different, but inside, the modifications

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.020
will be major.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:43.750
The total revamp starts with the removal of
the van's middle and third row seats.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:50.050
They pull out the carpet and completely
strip the vehicle from the front seats to the

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:54.820
back. Underneath.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.680
A technician saws off the exhaust system and
disassembles the rear suspension.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:05.470
The entire undercarriage behind the engine
is about to be reconfigured to make way for a

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.060
new lowered floor.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.480
The team also removes the 76 litre fuel
tank.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.400
They'll replace it with one of the same
capacity to fit the new configuration.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:20.770
They cut out a large section of floor and
relocate displaced wiring.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.510
They're now ready to roll the new floor
framework into position below the vehicle.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.650
This steel structure replaces the original
one and is strong enough to support the

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.480
additional weight of a wheelchair.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.510
A motorized one can weigh as much as four
adults.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:46.750
They slide the new steel floor into the
vehicle and lower it into the framework.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.300
This new floor is significantly lower than
the one the van came with, and it increases

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.640
the wheelchair passengers headroom by 30 to
40cm.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:04.960
After installing the new fuel tank,
suspension and exhaust systems, they returned

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.040
to the interior.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:10.180
A technician fits and bonds carpet under
padding to the van.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:16.810
He installs the carpet and clamps it while
the adhesive cures.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:24.070
With the back of the van rebuilt and
carpeted, they now ready the wheelchair ramp.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:28.080
A technician applies a gritty material with
an adhesive backing.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:32.290
He simply presses it to the ramp and smooths
out any air bubbles.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.630
This gritty material will provide a slip
resistant surface for the wheelchair to move

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:47.430
across. He attaches a handle and installs
two cable assemblies to connect the handle to

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.750
the ramp latches.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:58.080
He attaches a return spring to the handle
assembly.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:06.570
He turns the handle and tests the latches to
confirm that the mechanisms function

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:15.810
properly. The team then links the wheelchair
ramp to the new floor with a long

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:24.900
hinge. They install stainless steel trim
over the hinge to protect it and also connect

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.030
the counterbalance cables to the ramp.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:33.800
Hidden springs at the end of the cables
provide tension to significantly lighten the

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.210
load for the operator.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:42.470
The ramp weighs 60kg, but the operator ends
up lifting just seven kilograms of that due

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:44.000
to the spring loaded tension.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:50.260
They reinstall the interior plastic panels
and repurposed the original second row seat

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:59.010
belts. As they finish up inside, an employee
puts the company name on the door of the

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:06.520
vehicle. The wheelchair converted van is now
ready for a test drive.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.280
The operator unfolds the ramp.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.570
He folds away the new seats to make way for
the wheelchair.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:23.810
He extends the tie down belts and attaches
them to the wheelchair.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:36.110
He wheels the passenger into the van, locks
the wheelchair brakes, and ties the chair to

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:46.000
the floor. There are standard seatbelts to
fasten and then it's time to sit

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.740
back and enjoy the ride.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:52.960
Ensconced in the converted minivan, the
limited mobility individual can now

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.150
experience the freedom of the road.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:03.160
Bowls have existed for thousands of years.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:05.920
Very early bowls used to serve food or
drink.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:10.930
Have been found in China, ancient Greece,
and in certain North American cultures.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:17.800
Modern bowls can be made from a variety of
materials such as ceramic, plastic, metal and

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:23.620
wood. Wood bowls come in many shapes and
sizes.

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:29.650
Many different kinds of wood are used to
make bowls such as rubber wood and teak.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:34.440
Acacia is also a type of wood used to make
bowls.

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:40.830
Workers cut flat planks of acacia using a
large circular saw.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.860
The acacia was cut a few days ago in nearby
plantations.

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:52.540
Planks are cut to thicknesses required for
bowl production and pre dried on pallets

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:55.340
spaced apart for better air circulation.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.190
Using a table, saw a worker cuts the wood
planks.

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:04.930
Now kiln dried into pieces long enough to
make the bowl wall.

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:12.190
Another worker uses a tilted table saw to
cut the wood into shapes resembling a cone.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:16.300
Drying the wood eliminates water that might
cause cracking later on.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:24.050
A worker trims the pieces, giving them the
angle they need so the staves can be rounded

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:32.630
later on. Another worker smooths the rough
side of the staves with a spinning cutter.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:35.660
This also helps shape the wood pieces.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.980
Another employee lines up the staves on a
table before applying glue.

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:49.400
She spreads a wood bonding glue onto the
sides of the staves.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:55.100
Once they're thoroughly covered with glue,
she begins assembling the bowl by sticking

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:56.300
the sides together.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.230
She assembles a few pieces at a time,
handing them to another worker who arranges

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:07.890
the bowl shape by leaning the staves against
a centerpiece.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:14.260
They continue the process until all the
staves are in place.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:17.140
Then they'll squeeze the staves tightly
together.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:24.630
They stretch elastic bands around the
outside of the eventual bowl.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:30.990
The elastics held in place by a steel ring
squeeze the staves firmly against each other.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:33.570
With a hammer.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:38.430
She lightly taps the staves into place,
making sure they are flush against each

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:44.610
other. She turns the bowl over to make sure
none of the staves around the top rim are

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:46.050
sticking out too far.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:51.060
She then leaves the bowl to dry about five
hours later.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:56.550
They remove the elastics and again leave the
bowl to harden for a full day.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:02.370
A template is attached to the eventual bowl
in order to cut a circular opening for the

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:06.900
bottom piece. The template serves as a guide
during the cutting process.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:12.510
A worker moves the bowl around, allowing the
cutter to create a perfectly sized hole for

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:17.100
the bottom section of the bowl, which is
made of the same kind of wood, in this case,

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:23.790
acacia. A worker applies glue to the edges
of the bottom hole.

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:31.530
And to the outer edge of the round piece
that goes in the bottom of the bowl.

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:39.530
He inserts the bottom plate and uses a
hammer to force it firmly into place.

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:48.710
After another day of hardening, a worker
places the bowl in a lathe with a variety of

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:51.320
knives. He begins shaving off the wood.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.320
He starts on the inside wall of the bowl.

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:59.690
Then he shaves the outside of the bowl,
gradually giving it shape.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:05.250
He then uses sandpaper to smooth the surface
of the bowl.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:12.890
Another worker then uses a lathe to sand the
surface of the bowl.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.190
He makes the surface as fine as possible.

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:23.620
He must have a keen eye for detail in order
to make the bowl surface perfect.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:29.520
A worker sprays a foodstuff varnish onto the
surface of the bowl.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:34.050
Several layers are applied with drying and
polishing after each one.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:37.710
If required.

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:39.720
A stain is also applied.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:45.100
They place a sticker identifying the
manufacturer on the bottom of the bowl.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:50.830
A worker then packages the wood bowl with
the bowl secure.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:54.940
The only thing tossed about will be the
ingredients you put in it.

